  tools: ["MIG, TIG and stick welding machines", "Auto-darkening welding helmet", "Angle grinders", "Plasma and oxy-fuel cutting torches", "Clamps, squares and measuring tools", "Fume extraction equipment"],
  edu: "A high school diploma or GED is common. Many welders complete a technical school or community college program (months to two years); others learn through apprenticeships or on the job. Employers often require passing a welding test to their procedure or an AWS certification.",
  eduLevel: 2, train: "3–18 months", months: 6,
  certs: [["AWS Certified Welder", "Performance-based certification through the American Welding Society for specific processes and positions."], ["Structural welding (AWS D1.1)", "Common qualification for structural steel work."], ["ASME Section IX pipe qualification", "Often required for pressure piping and boilers."], ["Certified Welding Inspector (CWI)", "An advanced AWS credential for experienced welders who move into inspection."]],
  steps: [["Get a foundation", "High school shop classes, math and blueprint reading help."], ["Complete welding training", "Attend a technical school or community college program, or start as a helper in a shop."], ["Practice the main processes", "Build skill in MIG, stick, flux-core and TIG in multiple positions."], ["Pass a weld test or certification", "Employers test welders to specific procedures; AWS certifications prove your skill."], ["Specialize", "Pipe, structural, aerospace and underwater welding offer higher pay; inspection (CWI) is a strong next step."]],
